    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Conduct a bootstrap numerical experiment to test the phase transition hypothesis in solution space rigidity. First, establish a zero-violation anchor using a low-dimensional linear constraint problem (w=3, width=16, seed=42) to confirm feasibility. Then, systematically vary constraint density w (5,10,15,20,30) and measure the minimum network width N_c(w) that achieves zero violation (≤1e-3) on held-out constraints, using 3 random seeds per w. Analyze the trend of N_c(w) against w to infer rigidity (decreasing/flat) vs. fatness (increasing), with Hessian rank change as the theoretical marker of the critical point w_c."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom numerical experiment)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The zero-violation anchor will succeed with training violation=0.0 and held-out violation≤1e-3. N_c(w) will show a non-decreasing trend (likely increasing) with w, indicating a \"fat\" solution space with high-dimensional continuous families, not a rigid unique solution. The critical density w_c will be observed where N_c(w) begins to rise sharply, corresponding to the Hessian becoming singular as the constraint density exceeds the network's capacity to maintain a unique solution."
    },
